WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
Look along the spinal cord of a chick embryo.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:12.360
The sensory clusters called dorsal root ganglia sit in a neat, repeating row.

3
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.300
No central planner lays them out.

4
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.820
Their cells come from the neural crest, a population at the top of the neural tube.

5
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.420
So what turns a crowd of wandering cells into a row?

6
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:30.153
Crest cells begin as part of the tube's top edge.

7
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:32.873
They loosen their grip on their neighbours.

8
00:00:33.453 --> 00:00:35.153
Then they crawl away.

9
00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:42.613
That change, from a cell in a sheet to a free cell, is called an epithelial to

10
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:44.093
mesenchymal transition.

11
00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:47.593
Biologists shorten it to EMT.

12
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:54.907
In the chick trunk, one route, called ventromedial, runs through the somites.

13
00:00:56.027 --> 00:00:58.967
Somites are the blocks of tissue beside the tube.

14
00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.127
The part of each block that will form vertebrae is the sclerotome.

15
00:01:04.747 --> 00:01:09.807
It has a front half, called rostral, and a back half, called caudal.

16
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:13.927
Crest cells pass only through the rostral half.

17
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.090
The caudal half keeps them out.

18
00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.030
Crest cells carry a receptor called EphB.

19
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.490
Cells of the caudal half carry a signal on their surface, called ephrin-B.

20
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.210
Because it sits in the membrane, it works only when cells touch.

21
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.790
When a crest cell touches ephrin-B, the signal into the crest cell repels it, and it

22
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:38.790
is pushed away.

23
00:01:39.383 --> 00:01:41.883
Researchers tested this in a dish.

24
00:01:42.663 --> 00:01:48.303
They laid alternating stripes of ephrin-B and a control protein, and plated trunk

25
00:01:48.303 --> 00:01:49.503
crest cells on them.

26
00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.383
The cells avoided the ephrin-B stripes.

27
00:01:53.343 --> 00:01:56.703
A sceptic could say the stripe is just a poor surface.

28
00:01:57.323 --> 00:02:01.843
So they added soluble ephrin-B to the medium, meaning free in the liquid.

29
00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:03.963
That blocked the avoidance.

30
00:02:04.823 --> 00:02:07.783
The avoidance needs Eph and ephrin signaling.

31
00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:12.057
Back in the embryo, the same rule applies.

32
00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:17.937
Crest cells enter the rostral half of each sclerotome and not the caudal half.

33
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:22.517
That is one reason the dorsal root ganglia are segmented.

34
00:02:23.357 --> 00:02:25.617
Cells are not only drawn toward their targets.

35
00:02:26.277 --> 00:02:29.777
Where crest cells may not go helps shape what they build.
